import{_ as a,o,c as t,a2 as r}from"./chunks/framework.yZB1U4Nl.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"FAQ","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"faq.md","filePath":"faq.md"}'),n={name:"faq.md"};function s(i,e,h,d,l,u){return o(),t("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<h1 id="faq" tabindex="-1">FAQ <a class="header-anchor" href="#faq" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;FAQ&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Quick answers and common objections. For the full argument see <a href="./why-vlo">why-vlo.md</a>.</p><h2 id="choosing-vlo" tabindex="-1">Choosing Vlo <a class="header-anchor" href="#choosing-vlo" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Choosing Vlo&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="what-is-vlo-in-one-sentence" tabindex="-1">What is Vlo, in one sentence? <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-is-vlo-in-one-sentence" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What is Vlo, in one sentence?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>An open-source durable execution engine for workflows and AI agents that runs as a library on your own Postgres or a SQLite file.</p><h3 id="what-do-i-need-to-run-it" tabindex="-1">What do I need to run it? <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-do-i-need-to-run-it" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What do I need to run it?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Node 22.5 or later and a database: Postgres for production, a SQLite file for a laptop, CI or a small single-process host. There is no server, broker or cluster to install.</p><h3 id="i-want-to-write-plain-await-code-can-i" tabindex="-1">I want to write plain <code>await</code> code. Can I? <a class="header-anchor" href="#i-want-to-write-plain-await-code-can-i" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;I want to write plain `await` code. Can I?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>No, and that is the one real trade. A Vlo workflow is a reducer, not a function that pauses. The <code>sequence</code> helper makes pipelines read as a list of steps, and <code>agentLoop</code> builds an agent in one call. If straight <code>await a(); await b();</code> code matters most to your team, a replay-based engine is the better fit.</p><h3 id="reducers-sound-hard-are-they" tabindex="-1">Reducers sound hard. Are they? <a class="header-anchor" href="#reducers-sound-hard-are-they" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Reducers sound hard. Are they?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The hello-world workflow is about ten lines. Most workflows are a <code>sequence</code> and never touch a reducer directly. When you do write one, it is a plain function you can unit test with no engine running. See <a href="./testing">testing.md</a>.</p><h3 id="is-it-production-ready" tabindex="-1">Is it production-ready? <a class="header-anchor" href="#is-it-production-ready" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Is it production-ready?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>It is young, and it says so. The kernel is small enough to audit, tested with property tests and golden fixtures, and every checkpoint is hash-checked on load. It has no console, no managed cloud and no benchmark at scale. Read <a href="./status">status.md</a> and <a href="./limits">limits.md</a> before you commit to it.</p><h3 id="is-there-a-ui" tabindex="-1">Is there a UI? <a class="header-anchor" href="#is-there-a-ui" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Is there a UI?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Not yet. The journal is a table you can query, every run has a live event stream, and the HTTP API and CLI cover the rest. See <a href="./observability">observability.md</a>.</p><h3 id="what-about-lock-in" tabindex="-1">What about lock-in? <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-about-lock-in" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What about lock-in?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Vlo is Apache-2.0. The state is rows in your database, on your cloud. Nothing is hosted for you.</p><h3 id="does-it-run-on-the-edge" tabindex="-1">Does it run on the edge? <a class="header-anchor" href="#does-it-run-on-the-edge" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Does it run on the edge?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Not yet. The kernel is built for it: a wasm module with no I/O. The host and journals need Node today, and the Cloudflare host is the next platform milestone. See <a href="./status#about-built-for-the-edge">status.md</a>.</p><h2 id="performance-and-scale" tabindex="-1">Performance and scale <a class="header-anchor" href="#performance-and-scale" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Performance and scale&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="how-fast-is-it" tabindex="-1">How fast is it? <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-fast-is-it" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How fast is it?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>On a laptop with local Postgres, a 3-step workflow finishes in under 8 ms and a resume takes about 10 ms whether the run has 38 events or 20,000. Those are local numbers with no network. See <a href="./performance">performance.md</a> for the setup and the caveats.</p><h3 id="is-my-database-fast-enough-to-be-the-queue" tabindex="-1">Is my database fast enough to be the queue? <a class="header-anchor" href="#is-my-database-fast-enough-to-be-the-queue" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Is my database fast enough to be the queue?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>For most products, yes: the design is bounded by the database, in the low thousands of tasks per second on Postgres. If you already run a broker, it can carry tasks as a delivery transport, and the journal still records everything.</p><h3 id="where-should-the-host-run" tabindex="-1">Where should the host run? <a class="header-anchor" href="#where-should-the-host-run" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Where should the host run?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>In the same region as its journal. One step makes about 15 database round trips, so a slow link to the database makes every step slow.</p><h3 id="can-one-run-fan-out-to-thousands-of-tasks" tabindex="-1">Can one run fan out to thousands of tasks? <a class="header-anchor" href="#can-one-run-fan-out-to-thousands-of-tasks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Can one run fan out to thousands of tasks?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Use child runs for that. One run with very many tasks slows down sharply; <code>fanOutRuns</code> gives each item its own run and avoids the problem. See <a href="./limits#known-weak-spots">limits.md</a>.</p><h3 id="how-much-does-a-waiting-run-cost" tabindex="-1">How much does a waiting run cost? <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-much-does-a-waiting-run-cost" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How much does a waiting run cost?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>About 2 KB of rows on Postgres and no compute. A run waiting two days for an approval holds no process, no connection and no stack.</p><h2 id="building" tabindex="-1">Building <a class="header-anchor" href="#building" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Building&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="can-i-write-workflows-in-python-or-go" tabindex="-1">Can I write workflows in Python or Go? <a class="header-anchor" href="#can-i-write-workflows-in-python-or-go" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Can I write workflows in Python or Go?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Not yet. Workflows are TypeScript. Workers can be written in any language that can serve or call HTTP. See <a href="./recipes#write-a-worker-in-another-language">recipes.md</a>.</p><h3 id="which-model-providers-does-the-agent-layer-support" tabindex="-1">Which model providers does the agent layer support? <a class="header-anchor" href="#which-model-providers-does-the-agent-layer-support" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Which model providers does the agent layer support?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>All of them, because it calls none of them. Your worker makes the model call and reports what it spent. See <a href="./agent-harness">agent-harness.md</a>.</p><h3 id="what-happens-when-an-agent-runs-out-of-budget" tabindex="-1">What happens when an agent runs out of budget? <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-happens-when-an-agent-runs-out-of-budget" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What happens when an agent runs out of budget?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The run parks. Its status becomes <code>awaiting_signal</code>, it keeps its memory and its best result, and it resumes when someone sends a top-up. It does not fail and it does not keep spending. See <a href="./agents#parking-and-top-ups">agents.md</a>.</p><h3 id="can-an-agent-ask-a-person-before-it-does-something-risky" tabindex="-1">Can an agent ask a person before it does something risky? <a class="header-anchor" href="#can-an-agent-ask-a-person-before-it-does-something-risky" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Can an agent ask a person before it does something risky?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Yes. Name the tools that need approval in <code>agentLoop({ approval })</code>. When the model picks one, the run waits without holding compute until someone approves or rejects it, by API call or by a signed link. A rejection goes back to the model as an observation. See <a href="./agents#approving-tool-calls">agents.md</a>.</p><h3 id="how-long-can-a-task-run" tabindex="-1">How long can a task run? <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-long-can-a-task-run" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How long can a task run?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>As long as it needs. A task is held by a lease that the worker renews, so there is no broker deadline. Set <code>timeoutMs</code> only if you want a ceiling.</p><h3 id="will-my-handler-ever-run-twice" tabindex="-1">Will my handler ever run twice? <a class="header-anchor" href="#will-my-handler-ever-run-twice" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Will my handler ever run twice?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>It can. Execution is at-least-once and recording is exactly-once. Make a handler idempotent on <code>ctx.taskId</code> and <code>ctx.attempt</code> when its side effect matters. See <a href="./reliability#exactly-once-recording-at-least-once-execution">reliability.md</a>.</p><h3 id="can-i-change-a-workflow-while-runs-are-in-flight" tabindex="-1">Can I change a workflow while runs are in flight? <a class="header-anchor" href="#can-i-change-a-workflow-while-runs-are-in-flight" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Can I change a workflow while runs are in flight?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Carefully. A run pins the workflow&#39;s name, not its code. If you change a reducer&#39;s logic and a live run is later rebuilt from its journal, the rebuild no longer matches and the run fails with <code>nondeterminism_detected</code>. Let in-flight runs finish, or register the changed workflow under a new name. See <a href="./workflows#determinism">workflows.md</a>.</p><h3 id="what-must-a-reducer-not-do" tabindex="-1">What must a reducer not do? <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-must-a-reducer-not-do" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What must a reducer not do?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Read the clock, generate randomness, or do I/O. Time and entropy arrive on the event as <code>ts_ms</code> and <code>random</code>; everything else belongs in an activity. Nothing enforces this yet, so treat it as a rule of the road.</p><h3 id="how-do-i-test-a-workflow" tabindex="-1">How do I test a workflow? <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-do-i-test-a-workflow" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How do I test a workflow?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Call <code>workflow.reduce</code> directly for unit tests, or start an in-process host on a throwaway SQLite journal for end-to-end tests. See <a href="./testing">testing.md</a>.</p><h2 id="operating" tabindex="-1">Operating <a class="header-anchor" href="#operating" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Operating&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="does-it-work-on-serverless" tabindex="-1">Does it work on serverless? <a class="header-anchor" href="#does-it-work-on-serverless" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Does it work on serverless?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Yes. The host runs with a manual dispatcher: the request that starts or signals a run drains it, and a cron tick is the backstop. <code>vlo deploy</code> renders the files for Vercel and Cloud Run. See <a href="./deployment#serverless-manual-dispatch">deployment.md</a>.</p><h3 id="does-it-work-behind-a-connection-pooler" tabindex="-1">Does it work behind a connection pooler? <a class="header-anchor" href="#does-it-work-behind-a-connection-pooler" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Does it work behind a connection pooler?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Yes, including transaction-mode poolers. The Postgres journal keeps no session state. See <a href="./journal#postgres-adapter">journal.md</a>.</p><h3 id="how-do-i-see-what-a-run-did" tabindex="-1">How do I see what a run did? <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-do-i-see-what-a-run-did" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How do I see what a run did?&quot;">​</a></h3><p><code>GET /v1/runs/{id}/events</code> or <code>client.events(runId)</code> returns the journal: every step, attempt, retry, cause, error and usage figure. See <a href="./observability">observability.md</a>.</p><h3 id="a-run-failed-with-nondeterminism-detected-what-now" tabindex="-1">A run failed with <code>nondeterminism_detected</code>. What now? <a class="header-anchor" href="#a-run-failed-with-nondeterminism-detected-what-now" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A run failed with `nondeterminism_detected`. What now?&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Treat it as a bug report. A reducer no longer reproduces the history it wrote, usually because it read a clock or random value, or its logic changed under a live run. The error names the event where it diverged. See <a href="./errors">errors.md</a>.</p><h3 id="something-is-misconfigured-where-do-i-start" tabindex="-1">Something is misconfigured. Where do I start? <a class="header-anchor" href="#something-is-misconfigured-where-do-i-start" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Something is misconfigured. Where do I start?&quot;">​</a></h3><p><code>vlo doctor --profile &lt;name&gt;</code>. It runs real round trips against the journal, the blob store, the kernel and each queue, and prints a hint for anything that fails. See <a href="./cli">cli.md</a>.</p>',60)])])}const w=a(n,[["render",s]]);export{p as __pageData,w as default};
